At-a-glance summary (for your context only — the user has not seen any output yet):
# Claude Code Insights

315 sessions total · 105 analyzed · 1,955 messages · 359h · 526 commits
2026-07-03 to 2026-08-08

## At a Glance

**What's working:** You run Claude as an autonomous shipping engine — 'go do all,' 'finish everything from the last session' — and it consistently closes out multi-PR merge trains with prod verification and a handoff doc. What really distinguishes you is that you refuse to accept 'it works': you demand mutation-verified tests, live screenshots, and red→green reproducers, which repeatedly forced Claude to catch its own false verification claims. You also push back on shortcuts — rejecting model-pinning as a workaround and calling out a 100-line SQL mirror as slop — and both times the resulting design was materially better. See _Impressive Things You Did_.

**What's hindering you:** On Claude's side, the two biggest drags are self-introduced bugs (cross-tenant writes, a session-cookie regression, vacuous tests) that surface only when CI or a reviewer catches them, and a tendency to launch dozens of shell commands when you asked a simple knowledge question — twice you interrupted before getting any answer at all. On your side, sessions often stall on gates Claude structurally cannot clear: UI-proof artifacts, /ui-approve comments, admin merge rights, Actions outages, and exhausted reviewer quota. Concurrent sessions working the same PR queue also caused collisions. See _Where Things Go Wrong_.

**Quick wins to try:** Add a Hook on PostToolUse or pre-PR to auto-run the full integration suite and a tenancy/auth diff — that catches the class of bug reviewers are catching for you today. Turn your merge-train routine into a Custom Skill: claim PR ownership up front, treat CI-infra flakes as a stop condition after one retry, and end with a fixed-shape handoff file. For quick factual questions, say 'answer from knowledge, no tools' to skip the exploration loop entirely. See _Features to Try_.

